# ASR / Audio-LLM Domain Rules

Distilled from: Aksenova et al. 2021 "How Might We Create Better Benchmarks for Speech Recognition?" (ACL bppf), the ASR Leaderboard 2025 paper (arXiv 2510.06961), Whisper paper (Radford et al. 2023), Step-Audio 2 / Kimi-Audio / Seed-ASR / Qwen-Audio technical reports, NeurIPS 2026 evaluations & datasets call.

## 1. Reporting WER (and friends)

- Never report a single WER without specifying:
  - dataset name AND split (e.g. `LibriSpeech test-clean`, not just `LibriSpeech`)
  - normalization pipeline (e.g. "Whisper English normalizer", or describe rules: lowercase, expand contractions, strip punctuation, number normalization)
  - decoding configuration (greedy vs beam-N, temperature, length penalty, max new tokens)
  - whether you re-ran the baseline yourself or copied the number from a paper (cite both ways)
- The same normalization MUST be applied to both reference and hypothesis, and MUST be the same across compared systems. If you can't apply identical normalization across rows in a table, split the table or annotate with a footnote.
- Report at least one slice in addition to the average: per-language for multilingual, per-domain for multi-domain, per-noise-condition for robustness, U-WER vs B-WER for contextual ASR. A single average WER is necessary but never sufficient.
- If reporting CER, MER, BLEU, COMET (for translation), or named-entity F1, define the metric in a footnote at first use.
- Headline evidence must cover the breadth of the headline claim. Do not use only one or two convenient clean sets to support a claim of broad public-benchmark quality; show a representative multi-dataset view or explicitly narrow the claim.
- A macro average is allowed only across compatible metrics and protocols. Never average WER with CER, or rows with different normalisation / decoding rules. Always retain the per-dataset values beside any aggregate so the average cannot hide a weak slice.

## 2. Baselines and comparisons

- For every baseline number you cite or include in a table, you must specify one of:
  - "as reported in [cite]" with the cite
  - "we re-ran with [config]" with brief reproduction details
  - "from public leaderboard [URL] retrieved on [date]"
- Strongly prefer re-running with your own normalizer for the headline table; cite-as-reported numbers belong in a separate appendix table titled "Comparison to numbers reported in prior work".
- Required baselines for an English LLM-ASR report: Whisper-large-v3 (must), at least one strong recent open model (Canary / Parakeet / OWSM v3.1 / SeamlessM4T), and your prior internal version if any.
- For a model adapted from a public backbone, add a capability-delta audit before writing the Abstract: inherited languages/tasks, retained capabilities, intentionally narrowed scope, and capabilities newly introduced by this work. Do not present an inherited or reduced capability as the paper's novelty.

## 3. Data section requirements

How much of the data recipe to disclose is a per-report decision. A report may withhold sampling weights, per-source hours or counts, mixing probabilities and parameter ranges, and cleaning thresholds.

Every report still states:

- the public corpora used and what each is used for, with citations;
- the categories of data and, at the level of concepts, how they are constructed;
- that non-public data are used, if they are, so that a list of public corpora is not read as complete;
- how training and evaluation data are separated, wherever overlap affects how results should be read.

Anything a report does disclose must be accurate and traceable. A number used as evidence for a claim keeps its source.

If a report does give a per-source breakdown, each row carries name, hours (say whether after VAD filtering), number of utterances or segments, language(s), license and whether redistributable, domain category, release year and citation, and the table sums to a clear "Total" row. A released dataset also follows `reproducibility.mdc` §2, whatever the report withholds.

## 4. Model / architecture description

For every component, report:

- module name (Encoder / Adapter / LLM)
- backbone identity and version (e.g. `Whisper-large-v3 encoder`, `Qwen2.5-7B-Base`)
- parameter count (millions or billions, with one decimal)
- hidden dimension, number of layers, attention heads
- frame rate (Hz) at the boundary between modules
- which weights are frozen / trainable / LoRA-adapted in each training stage

Do not paste literal prompt templates; they are implementation detail (AGENTS.md rule 4). State what each task's prompt carries, for example "a list of hotwords" or "a fixed instruction".

Prompt provenance is still part of the model specification:

- Verify each description against the final training/evaluation source of truth, not memory or a nearby model's README.
- Say whether each task's prompt is inherited unchanged, modified from the backbone, or introduced by this work; cite the public backbone at the first inherited prompt.
- If the official public interface hides an internal template, do not attribute a guessed form to the backbone. Mark any unresolved provenance with `% TODO: verify`.

For contextual / hotword ASR, keep two layers distinct:

- retrieval layer: recalls/selects candidate hotwords from the external vocabulary;
- conditioning layer: encodes/injects the selected terms and changes the ASR model's transcription behaviour.

Report and diagram both layers. Retriever Recall@$K$ alone does not establish contextual-awareness quality; pair it with B-WER/U-WER or another end-to-end recognition measure.

## 5. Training section requirements

How much of the training setup to disclose is a per-report decision. A report may withhold hardware, learning-rate schedules, batch sizes, step counts and other hyperparameters.

Every report still states the initialization, the training stages and what each one is for, which parameters are trained or frozen, and the objective in words.

If a report does give details:

- Give the objective with its formula on first use.
- Per stage: trainable params, learning rate schedule, batch size in audio-hours and in tokens, sequence length cap, total tokens consumed, total update steps, precision (fp32 / bf16 / fp16-amp), optimizer (AdamW betas, weight decay).
- Hardware: GPU/TPU type and count, parallelism strategy (FSDP / ZeRO-3 / TP+PP), throughput in TFLOPS or audio-hours/GPU/day, total wall-clock training time.
- If RL / DPO is used: preference data source, pair construction policy, KL coefficient.
- Distinguish the decoding algorithm from the serving backend. Always report greedy/beam/sampling parameters, because they affect the metric; name vLLM or another server only when it changes decoding semantics or supports a measured systems claim such as throughput or latency.

## 6. Robustness, long-form, streaming

If the model claims any of the following, you must include the corresponding evaluation:

- "Robust to noise" → SNR sweep on a noise-augmented dev set with at least 3 SNR levels.
- "Long-form" → numbers on at least one long-form benchmark (TED-LIUM 3 long-form, GigaSpeech long, AMI, Earnings22, or a corpus of ≥30 min recordings).
- "Streaming" → measured first-token latency and real-time factor with the streaming setting actually used.
- "Multilingual" → FLEURS or similar multilingual benchmark with per-language-group averages.
- "Code-switch" → at least one benchmark like SEAME or ASCEND.

If the model does not claim a property, it is acceptable to omit the corresponding evaluation, but say so explicitly in Limitations.

## 7. Failure modes (must include)

LLM-based ASR has well-documented failure modes — be honest:

- Repetition / hallucination loops on silence or noise.
- Language drift under code-switching.
- Rare named-entity errors (especially without contextual biasing).
- Sensitivity to the system prompt.
- Catastrophic forgetting of the LLM's text capabilities after speech fine-tuning.

Show one qualitative example per claimed failure mode if making the claim.

## 8. Historical framing and task introduction

- If the Introduction uses an ASR timeline, include the intermediate paradigm(s) needed for the argument (for example CTC, RNN-T, or attention-based encoder-decoder systems between hybrid HMM-DNN and SpeechLLMs), with citations. Otherwise compress the sentence into a non-historical contrast instead of presenting a misleading leap.
- Define task semantics before reporting task results: what side information is provided, what speech must be transcribed, and what counts as success. This is mandatory for target-speaker ASR, contextual ASR, code-switching, whispered speech, and degradation robustness.
